[12:36] expr04

I have been working on the generation of examples from the grammar.
The examples should have a scenario to model and a reference solution.

The way I have set it up is that I am asking the llm to generate an
exam question. The issue I found is that it is difficult to get the
model to create widely varied examples.

To solve that I use wordnet to generate random nouns and verbs that I
ask it to use in the example. I also used copilot to find other ways
of varying the examples with difficulty, learning objective, and
structural profile.

All errors are propogated to the model and it has 5 tries to get
output an example.

A side note is that I like this way of working (for this type of task)
where I set up a skeleton of the problem by hand and then I use
copilot to look at the output and suggest changes. If I like the
changes it suggests I get it to implement the changes. If I see a new
issue in the output I ask copilot to look at the output again and ask
it to consider the issue and suggest new changes.

The code I am writing is mostly throw-away, but I still need to have a
good grasp of the underlaying ideas. Working in this way it is easier
to understand the additions that copilot adds.



