* [2026-07-16 Thu]

[16:03]

There is an issue using diffusion text models and grammars. The
[[https://github.com/mlc-ai/xgrammar][xgrammar]] library assumes that generation is one token at a time--like
a transformer does--but the diffusion models generate a block at a
time. I need a way to say how the text is _close_ to the grammar.

I have been thinking about a couple of different ideas:
    1. Just sample and validate the grammar
    2. Do the same on a line basis
    3. Evaluate each token as an anchor

The issue with 1 is that most texts will be bad and I fear I will just
end up in a local minimum that is far away from anything useful as it
has to find a small step to be able to train at all. Inefficient.

2 solves some of this--but feels wrong. I want to be able to say
something about each "prediction".

I think 3 is the correct direction--what if for any given token, we
assumed that it could be anywhere in the grammar. Which tokens could
it be? If that is right, what tokens could then it's neighbours be? Do
this until we hit some limit:
  - Artificial set k?
  - Finished the current statement?
  - Found a whole program?

How do we sum or multiply the belief out afterwards to update the
model? Can we use the same structures to resolve the final output at
the end? Like they do in scallop and dolphin?

My worry is that it can be slow. How is the sampling done and what can
we do to speed it up?

